#{[17592186654298 "We will determine each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,2),$ we have $f(x)=-1.$ Notice that the point $x=2$ is excluded.\n\n- On the interval $[2,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=1.$ Notice that the point $x=2$ is included.\n\nHence the definition of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1\\,,\\, & x<2 \\\\ 1\\,,\\, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654306 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,-2],$ we have a line with a slope of $-4$ that passes through the point $(-2,-2).$ We can find the equation of this line using the point-slope form:\n\n$$\ny-(-2)=-4(x-(-2))\\,⟹\\,y=-4x-10\n$$\n\nSo the graph is the line $y=-4x-10$ that ends at $(-2,-2),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$ we have a line with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $0.$ So the graph is the line $y=-x$ that starts at $(-2,2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x-10,\\, & x\\le -2 \\\\ -x,\\, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654325 "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,-1],$ we have $f(x)=-1,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(-1,-1),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(-1,\\infty ),$ we have a line with a slope of $2$ and $y$-intercept $0.$ So the graph is the line $y=2x$ that starts at $(-1,-2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the function is defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2x,\\, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654746 "The marginal density function for $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186654771 "The marginal density function for $X$ when $0\\le x\\le 2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+3{y}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[xy+{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4}, & 0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the  expected value of $X$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot \\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{8}{3}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186655513 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.63),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.63, & x=1 \\\\ 0.37, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.37.\n$$"] [17592186656870 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$  \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}, & \\,-1\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $-1\\le x\\le 1,-1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}, & \\,-1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<Y<\\frac{3}{2}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{6}) & =P(\\frac{1}{6}<Y<\\frac{3}{2}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/6}^{3/2}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{1}{6})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/6}^{3/2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{3}}{9}]}_{1/6}^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{27}{8}-\\frac{1}{216}) \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{243}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656896 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8{x}^{3}y}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2y}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656917 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+y}{\\left(\\frac{2y+1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+2y}{2y+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2y}{2y+1}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656922 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{15{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}{7}\\right)}{5{y}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{7}, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656924 "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3({x}^{2}+y)}{8}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[y{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}[2{x}^{2}+2-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)}{4}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3({x}^{2}+y)}{8}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3({x}^{2}+1)}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{2({x}^{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{2({x}^{2}+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) & =P(Y<\\frac{1}{3}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{({\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+y)}{2({\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+1)}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{\\left(\\frac{9y+1}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{20}{9}\\right)}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}(9y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}{[\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\cdot \\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656937 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le y,\\,0<y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le y,\\,0<y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656945 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,{|}_{1}^{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}, & \\,1\\le y\\le e \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le {e}^{2},\\,1\\le y\\le e,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{xy}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}{y}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2x}, & \\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{2},\\,1\\le y\\le e \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656947 "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}]}_{y=-1}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}-[-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+1}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{2(3{x}^{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}{2(3{x}^{2}+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186656950 "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nFrom the equation above, it follows that\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})}\\cdot 12{x}^{3}(1-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =24xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ equals $0.$ As a result, the product will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the joint probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}24xy, & 0<x<1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186658253 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-4,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge -2]=1[-4\\ge -2]=0$ (since $-4\\ge -2$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-2]=1[-4<-2]=1$ (since $-4<-2$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =1[-4\\ge -2]\\cdot (-4)^{2}+1[-4<-2]\\cdot (-(-4)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 16+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-4<-2,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result."] [17592186658256 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{G}(x)$ of a set $G$ equals $1$ if $x\\in G$ and $0$ if $x\\notin G.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{G}(x)=1[x\\in G].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{G}(-2)=1[-2\\in G]=1[-2\\in \\{-4,-2,0,6\\}].\n$$"] [17592186658272 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $-2>-5.$ Since $-2$ is greater than $-5,$ the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[-2>-5]=1.\n$$"] [17592186658278 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $-4<-10.$ Since $-4$ is not less than $-10,$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[-4<-10]=0.\n$$"] [17592186658297 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}+2)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot (-1)$ equals ${x}_{i}+2$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $-1$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}+2)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot (-1)\n$$\n\nadds ${x}_{i}+2$ for nonnegative values and $-1$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-2\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[5\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[-7\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[3\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}+2)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot (-1) & =(-1)+(5+2)+(-1)+(0+2)+(3+2) \\\\  & =-1+7-1+2+5 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186658309 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[2\\in G]$ has $x=2$ and $A=G.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[2\\in G]={1}_{G}(2).\n$$"] [17592186658314 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $-4\\in \\{-6,-4,0,9\\}.$ Since $-4$ is an element of the set $\\{-6,-4,0,9\\},$ the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[-4\\in \\{-6,-4,0,9\\}]=1.\n$$"] [17592186658322 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[-1\\in D]$ has $x=-1$ and $A=D.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[-1\\in D]={1}_{D}(-1).\n$$"] [17592186658326 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=2,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 3]=1[2\\ge 3]=0$ (since $2\\ge 3$ is false)\n\n- $1[0\\le x<3]=1[0\\le 2<3]=1$ (since $0\\le 2<3$ is true)\n\n- $1[x<0]=1[2<0]=0$ (since $2<0$ is false)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $q(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(2) & =0\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $0\\le x<3,$ only the second term contributes to the result."] [17592186658915 "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $5,$ then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $-5,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9x+5y=4 \\\\ (\\,-2x-y=1\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}9x+5y &=4 \\\\ -10x-5y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}9x+\\cancel{5y} & =4 \\\\ -10x-\\cancel{5y} & =5 \\\\ \\hline (9x-10x) & =(4+5) \\\\ -x & =9 \\\\ x & =-9\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $y,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $5,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658920 "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-5,$ then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $-5,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3a+5b=17 \\\\ (\\,2a+b=9\\,)\\,\\times (-5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3a+5b &=17 \\\\ -10a-5b &=-45\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}3a+\\cancel{5b} & =17 \\\\ -10a-\\cancel{5b} & =-45 \\\\ \\hline (3a-10a) & =(17-45) \\\\ -7a & =-28\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $b,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-5,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658926 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $6,$ while in the second equation it is $-3.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $2,$ then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+5y=20 \\\\ (\\,-3x+4y=-23\\,)\\,\\times (2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x+5y &=20 \\\\ -6x+8y &=-46\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{6x}+5y & =20 \\\\ -\\cancel{6x}+8y & =-46 \\\\ \\hline (5y+8y) & =(20-46) \\\\ 13y & =-26\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $x,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $2,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658927 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{6,7,8,…,21\\},$ and the set $S=\\{6,7,8,…,21\\}$ has $|S|=21-6+1=16$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}, & x=6,7,8,…,21 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\ge 14$ are $14,15,16,…21.$ There are $21-14+1=8$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 14)=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186658931 "Notice that the coefficient of $p$ in the first equation is $7,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-7,$ then the coefficient of $p$ will turn into $-7,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $7$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7p-15q=-2 \\\\ (\\,p-6q=-8\\,)\\,\\times (-7)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7p-15q &=-2 \\\\ -7p+42q &=56\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $p$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{7p}-15q & =-2 \\\\ -\\cancel{7p}+42q & =56 \\\\ \\hline (-15q+42q) & =(-2+56) \\\\ 27q & =54\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*Multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $-7,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658933 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\},$ and the set $S=\\{-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\}$ has $|S|=7$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}, & x=-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$"] [17592186658934 "Notice that the $p$-coefficient in the first equation is $8,$ while in the second equation it is $-16.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then the $p$-coefficient will become $16,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-16$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,8p-6q=-20\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ -16p+7q=30\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}16p-12q &=-40 \\\\ -16p+7q &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $p$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{16p}-12q & =-40 \\\\ -\\cancel{16p}+7q & =30 \\\\ \\hline (-12q+7q) & =(-40+30) \\\\ -5q & =-10 \\\\ q & =2\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $q=2.$ However, we still need to find $p.$ To do this, we substitute $q=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8p-6q & =-20 \\\\ 8p-6(2) & =-20 \\\\ 8p-12 & =-20 \\\\ 8p & =-8 \\\\ p & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-1,\\,q=2,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,2).$"] [17592186658935 "Notice that the coefficient of $b$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $3.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-3,$ then the coefficient of $b$ will turn into $-3,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,a+b=-3\\,)\\,\\times (-3) \\\\ 2a+3b=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3a-3b &=9 \\\\ 2a+3b &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $b$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}-3a-\\cancel{3b} & =9 \\\\ 2a+\\cancel{3b} & =1 \\\\ \\hline (-3a+2a) & =(9+1) \\\\ -a & =10 \\\\ a & =-10\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $b,$ multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $-3,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658940 "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $3,$ while in the second equation it is $-1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $3,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $-3,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $3$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2u+3v=14 \\\\ (\\,3u-v=-14\\,)\\,\\times (3)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2u+3v &=14 \\\\ 9u-3v &=-42\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}-2u+\\cancel{3v} & =14 \\\\ 9u-\\cancel{3v} & =-42 \\\\ \\hline (-2u+9u) & =(14-42) \\\\ 7u & =-28 \\\\ u & =-4\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u=-4.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u=-4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3u-v & =-14 \\\\ 3(-4)-v & =-14 \\\\ -12-v & =-14 \\\\ -v & =-2 \\\\ v & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-4,\\,v=2,$ which can be expressed as $(-4,2).$"] [17592186658943 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{9,10,11,…,44\\},$ and the set $S=\\{9,10,11,…,44\\}$ has $|S|=44-9+1=36$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{36}, & x=9,10,11,…,44 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $11<X<30$ are $12,13,14,…,29.$ There are $29-12+1=18$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(11<X<30)=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{36}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186658944 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{2,4,6,7,8,9\\},$ and the set $S=\\{2,4,6,7,8,9\\}$ has $|S|=6$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}, & x=2,4,6,7,8,9 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ there are $2$ items $(6,7)$ that satisfy $6\\le X<8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(6\\le X<8)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186658952 "Notice that the $x$-coefficient in the first equation is $-2,$ while in the second equation it is $4.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-4,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $4$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-2x+5y=-11\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 4x+7y=5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x+10y &=-22 \\\\ 4x+7y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}-\\cancel{4x}+10y & =-22 \\\\ \\cancel{4x}+7y & =5 \\\\ \\hline (10y+7y) & =(-22+5) \\\\ 17y & =-17 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=-1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}4x+7y & =5 \\\\ 4x+7(-1) & =5 \\\\ 4x+-7 & =5 \\\\ 4x & =12 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3,\\,y=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(3,-1).$"] [17592186658958 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-5,$ while in the second equation it is $1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $5,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $5,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-5$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-3y=1 \\\\ (\\,x+2y=-3\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-3y &=1 \\\\ 5x+10y &=-15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}-\\cancel{5x}-3y & =1 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}+10y & =-15 \\\\ \\hline (-3y+10y) & =(1-15) \\\\ 7y & =-14 \\\\ y & =-2\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=-2.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=-2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+2y & =-3 \\\\ x+2(-2) & =-3 \\\\ x-4 & =-3 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=1,\\,y=-2,$ which can be expressed as $(1,-2).$"] [17592186658960 "Notice that the $q$-coefficient in the first equation is $3,$ while in the second equation it is $6.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-2,$ then the $q$-coefficient will become $-6,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,2p+3q=5\\,)\\,\\times (-2) \\\\ 7p+6q=13\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4p-6q &=-10 \\\\ 7p+6q &=13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}-4p-\\cancel{6q} & =-10 \\\\ 7p+\\cancel{6q} & =13 \\\\ \\hline (-4p+7p) & =(-10+13) \\\\ 3p & =3 \\\\ p & =1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $p=1.$ However, we still need to find $q.$ To do this, we substitute $p=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p+3q & =5 \\\\ 2(1)+3q & =5 \\\\ 2+3q & =5 \\\\ 3q & =3 \\\\ q & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=1,\\,q=1,$ which can be expressed as $(1,1).$"] [17592186658968 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $-6,$ while in the second equation it is $24.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $4,$ then the coefficient of $x$ will turn into $-24,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $24$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-6x+2y=2\\,)\\,\\times (4) \\\\ 24x-5y=-5\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-24x+8y &=8 \\\\ 24x-5y &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{-24x}+8y & =8 \\\\ \\cancel{24x}-5y & =-5 \\\\ \\hline (8y-5y) & =(8-5) \\\\ 3y & =3\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $x,$ multiply the $\\text{first}$ equation by $4,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658971 "Notice that the $v$-coefficient in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-10.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $2,$ then the $v$-coefficient will become $10,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $-10$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,3u+5v=2\\,)\\,\\times (2) \\\\ 7u-10v=-17\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6u+10v &=4 \\\\ 7u-10v &=-17\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $v$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}6u+\\cancel{10v} & =4 \\\\ 7u-\\cancel{10v} & =-17 \\\\ \\hline (6u+7u) & =(4-17) \\\\ 13u & =-13 \\\\ u & =-1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $u=-1.$ However, we still need to find $v.$ To do this, we substitute $u=-1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7u-10v & =-17 \\\\ 7(-1)-10v & =-17 \\\\ -7-10v & =-17 \\\\ -10v & =-10 \\\\ v & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-1,\\,v=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,1).$"] [17592186658972 "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is $8,$ while in the second equation it is $-2.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $4,$ then the coefficient of $q$ will turn into $-8,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $8$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9p+8q=-36 \\\\ (\\,p-2q=-4\\,)\\,\\times (4)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4p-8q &=-16 \\\\ 9p+8q &=-36\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}4p-\\cancel{8q} & =-16 \\\\ 9p+\\cancel{8q} & =-36 \\\\ \\hline (4p+9p) & =(-16-36) \\\\ 13p & =-52\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $q,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $4,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658973 "Notice that the coefficient of $p$ in the first equation is $5,$ while in the second equation it is $-1.$ \n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $5,$ then the coefficient of $p$ will turn into $-5,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5p+3q=7 \\\\ (\\,-p+2q=-4\\,)\\,\\times (5)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5p+3q &=7 \\\\ -5p+10q &=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $p$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{5p}+3q & =7 \\\\ -\\cancel{5p}+10q & =-20 \\\\ \\hline (3q+10q) & =(7-20) \\\\ 13q & =-13\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n*To eliminate $p,$ multiply the $\\text{second}$ equation by $5,$ and add the equations*."] [17592186658979 "Let $X$ represent the time a visitor spends on the webpage. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[1.5,4.5],$ and we wish to compute $P(X>2.5).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[1.5,4.5],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}, & 1.5\\le x\\le 4.5 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2.5) & ={\\int }_{2.5}^{4.5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2.5}^{4.5}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{3}|}_{2.5}^{4.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{3}-\\frac{2.5}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186658980 "Let $X$ represent the weight lost during the first month for a randomly chosen person following the diet. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[5,12],$ and we wish to compute $P(7\\le X<10).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[5,12],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}, & 5\\le x\\le 12 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7\\le X<10) & ={\\int }_{7}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{7}^{10}\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{7}|}_{7}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{7}-\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186658983 "Notice that the coefficient of $p$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $5.$ \n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-5,$ then the coefficient of $p$ will turn into $-5,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(p+q=6)\\,\\times (-5) \\\\ 5p-4q=12\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5p-5q &=-30 \\\\ 5p-4q &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $p$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}\\cancel{-5p}-5q & =-30 \\\\ \\cancel{5p}-4q & =12 \\\\ \\hline (-5q-4q) & =(-30+12) \\\\ -9q & =-18 \\\\ q & =2\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $q=2.$ However, we still need to find $p.$ To do this, we substitute $q=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+q & =6 \\\\ p+2 & =6 \\\\ p & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=4,\\,q=2,$ which can be expressed as $(4,2).$"] [17592186658987 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{-5,-4,-3,…,6\\},$ and the set $S=\\{-5,-4,-3,…,6\\}$ has $|S|=6-(-5)+1=12$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}, & x=-5,-4,-3,…,6 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn $S,$ the items that satisfy $X\\ge 3$ are $3,4,5,6.$ There are $4$ of these items. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=4\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186658990 "If $X$ follows a discrete uniform distribution over a set $S,$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{|S|}, & x\\in S \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼U\\{9,10,11,…,18\\},$ and the set $S=\\{9,10,11,…,18\\}$ has $|S|=18-9+1=10$ items. So, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}, & x=9,10,11,…,18 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=11)=f(11)=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$"] [17592186658994 "Let $X$ represent the height of water. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[165,290],$ and we wish to compute $P(X\\le 250).$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a}, & a\\le x\\le b \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[165,290],$ we have the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{125}, & 165\\le x\\le 290 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 250) & ={\\int }_{165}^{250}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{165}^{250}\\frac{1}{125}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{x}{125}|}_{165}^{250} \\\\  & =\\frac{250}{125}-\\frac{165}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186658995 "Notice that the coefficient of $x$ in the first equation is $1,$ while in the second equation it is $5.$\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $-5,$ then the $x$-coefficient will become $-5,$ which will cancel with the coefficient of $5$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,x+3y=7\\,)\\,\\times (-5) \\\\ 5x+4y=2\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x-15y &=-35 \\\\ 5x+4y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{rl}-\\cancel{5x}-15y & =-35 \\\\ \\cancel{5x}+4y & =2 \\\\ \\hline (-15y+4y) & =(-35+2) \\\\ -11y & =-33 \\\\ y & =3\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=3.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =7 \\\\ x+3(3) & =7 \\\\ x+9 & =7 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-2,\\,y=3,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,3).$"] [17592186659668 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=\\sqrt[3]{X}$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $1<x<8,$ we have $1<y<2.$\n\n![](images/21/21d19426e86916c123e898bf5427ef858784a601eedb861ba55e22855912005c.png)\n\n*Note: Image not drawn to scale.*\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $1<y<2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\sqrt[3]{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le {y}^{3}) \\\\  & =F({y}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $1<y<2,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{({y}^{3})^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 1<y<2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186659672 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=4X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $0<x<2,$ we have $0<y<8.$\n\n![](images/96/96df3a12ff46d4f07a12beb3ffb89bee427da352ee0e7b4c42dea3a19e923a5f.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(4X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y}{4}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<8,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\frac{y}{4}+1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{y+4}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{64}, & \\,0<y<8 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186659700 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=\\sqrt{X}$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<1.$\n\n![](images/26/26d742853a76c8af3ef1c55a6d6f728e38a3c891b4416bc4a80936968cfca76b.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\sqrt{X}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le {y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<1,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =2y\\cdot {f}_{X}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{{y}^{2}}}{2} \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2},\\, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186659715 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=2X+1$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$  Moreover, for $1<x<2,$ we have $3<y<5.$\n\n![](images/bb/bb2f32772d3bcf0ae72f5d015ec78ee096b1501d1a3c1b977e2d00720d0caf59.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $3<y<5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $3<y<5,$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{y-1}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(y-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(y-1)^{2}},\\, & 3<y<5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186659740 "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=-\\frac{1}{2}X$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $y\\le -\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c0/c073010347f3fb0d1922a7fa50737bd104f8c82fa1de7e4cf434586b1433d1ff.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $y\\le -\\frac{1}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{1}{2}X\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\ge -2y) \\\\  & =1-{F}_{X}(-2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $y\\le -\\frac{1}{2},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =0-(-2)\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(-2y) \\\\  & =2{f}_{X}(-2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =2{f}_{X}(-2y) \\\\  & =2{e}^{1-(-2y)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1+2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{1+2y}, & \\,y\\le -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660310 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=(1-X)^{3}.$ The transformation $u(x)=(1-x)^{3}$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =(1-y)^{3} \\\\ y & =(1-{u}^{-1}(y))^{3} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =1-\\sqrt[3]{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(1-\\sqrt[3]{y})\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}}| \\\\  & =3(1-[1-\\sqrt[3]{y}])^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=1,\\,u(1)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660344 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=\\frac{1}{3}X+1.$ The transformation $u(x)=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =\\frac{1}{3}y+1\\text{ } \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{-1}(y)+1 \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =3y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=3\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(3y-3)\\cdot |3| \\\\  & =3(3y-3)^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =81(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=1,\\,u(1)=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}81(y-1)^{2}, & 1<y<\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660347 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *decreasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={e}^{-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=-{e}^{-y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}({e}^{-y})\\cdot |-{e}^{-y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({e}^{-y})^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2y}}\\cdot {e}^{-y} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & y\\le 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660356 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y={e}^{X}.$ The transformation $u(x)={e}^{x}$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & ={e}^{y} \\\\ y & ={e}^{{u}^{-1}(y)} \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))\\cdot |\\frac{1}{y}| \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{|y|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}}{3|y|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $0<x<1,$ the bounds are $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(0)=1,\\,u(1)=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}}{3y}, & 1<y<e \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660358 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y={log}_{2}⁡X.$ The transformation $u(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & ={log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ y & ={log}_{2}⁡({u}^{-1}(y)) \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & ={2}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)={2}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {2}^{y}}{3}\\cdot {2}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $1<x<2,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,u(2)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{2}^{2y+1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}, & 0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660371 "If $X\\in (a,b)$ is a continuous random variable with probability density function ${f}_{X}$ and $Y=u(X),$ where the function $u(x)$ is strictly monotonic, then according to the change-of-variables method, the probability density function of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)|, & y\\text{ is between }u(a)\\text{ and }u(b) \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $Y=X-1.$ The transformation $u(x)=x-1$ is strictly monotonically *increasing*, so the change-of-variables method can be used.\n\nFirst, we find ${u}^{-1}(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(y) & =y-1 \\\\ y & ={u}^{-1}(y)-1 \\\\ {u}^{-1}(y) & =y+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find $({u}^{-1})^{′}(y):$\n\n$$\n({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)=1\n$$\n\nAccording to the change-of-variables method, for nonzero ${f}_{Y},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={f}_{X}({u}^{-1}(y))\\cdot |({u}^{-1})^{′}(y)| \\\\  & ={f}_{X}(y+1)\\cdot |1| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${f}_{X}$ is nonzero on $x\\ge 1,$ the bounds are $a=1$ and $b\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nu(1)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }u(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability distribution of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(y+1)^{2}}, & y>0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660875 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.4, & x=1 \\\\ 0.6, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.4.\n$$"] [17592186660882 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.32),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.32, & x=1 \\\\ 0.68, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.32.\n$$"] [17592186660915 "If $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.2),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.2, & x=1 \\\\ 0.8, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=0)=f(0)=0.8.\n$$"] [17592186661485 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(12,0.9),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{11})(0.1)^{x-12}(0.9)^{12}, & x=12,13,14,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=18) & =f(18) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18-1}{11})(0.1)^{18-12}(0.9)^{12} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{17}{11})(0.1)^{6}(0.9)^{12} \\\\  & ≈0.0035\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661490 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(12,0.44),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{11})(0.56)^{x-12}(0.44)^{12}, & x=12,13,14,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(17<X\\le 20) & =P(X\\in \\{18,19,20\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=18)+P(X=19)+P(X=20) \\\\  & =f(18)+f(19)+f(20) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{17}{11})(0.56)^{6}(0.44)^{12}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{11})(0.56)^{7}(0.44)^{12}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{19}{11})(0.56)^{8}(0.44)^{12} \\\\  & ≈0.0875\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661495 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(20,0.75),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{19})(0.25)^{x-20}(0.75)^{20}, & x=20,21,22,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<23) & =P(X\\in \\{20,21,22\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=20)+P(X=21)+P(X=22) \\\\  & =f(20)+f(21)+f(22) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{19}{19})(0.25)^{0}(0.75)^{20}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{19})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{20}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{21}{19})(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{20} \\\\  & ≈0.0606\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661498 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(7,0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{6})(0.6)^{x-7}(0.4)^{7}, & x=7,8,9,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=10) & =f(10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10-1}{6})(0.6)^{10-7}(0.4)^{7} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{6})(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0297\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661508 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(25,0.73),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{24})(0.27)^{x-25}(0.73)^{25}, & x=25,26,27,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 28.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 28) & =1-P(X<28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<28) & =P(X\\in \\{25,26,27\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=25)+P(X=26)+P(X=27) \\\\  & =f(25)+f(26)+f(27) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{24}{24})(0.27)^{0}(0.73)^{25}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{24})(0.27)^{1}(0.73)^{25}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{26}{24})(0.27)^{2}(0.73)^{25} \\\\  & ≈0.0120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 28) & =1-P(X<28) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0120 \\\\  & =0.9880\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661514 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(17,0.81),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{16})(0.19)^{x-17}(0.81)^{17}, & x=17,18,19,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(21<X<25) & =P(X\\in \\{22,23,24\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=22)+P(X=23)+P(X=24) \\\\  & =f(22)+f(23)+f(24) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{21}{16})(0.19)^{5}(0.81)^{17}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{22}{16})(0.19)^{6}(0.81)^{17}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{23}{16})(0.19)^{7}(0.81)^{17} \\\\  & ≈0.2987\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661522 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(9,0.7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{8})(0.3)^{x-9}(0.7)^{9}, & x=9,10,11,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=13) & =f(13) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13-1}{8})(0.3)^{13-9}(0.7)^{9} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{8})(0.3)^{4}(0.7)^{9} \\\\  & ≈0.1618\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661523 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(18,0.57),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{17})(0.43)^{x-18}(0.57)^{18}, & x=18,19,20,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>20.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20) & =1-P(X\\le 20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =P(X\\in \\{18,19,20\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=18)+P(X=19)+P(X=20) \\\\  & =f(18)+f(19)+f(20) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{17}{17})(0.43)^{0}(0.57)^{18}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{17})(0.43)^{1}(0.57)^{18}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{19}{17})(0.43)^{2}(0.57)^{18} \\\\  & ≈0.0016.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20) & =1-P(X\\le 20) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0016 \\\\  & =0.9984\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661527 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(9,0.33),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{8})(0.67)^{x-9}(0.33)^{9}, & x=9,10,11,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>11.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & =1-P(X\\le 11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 11) & =P(X\\in \\{9,10,11\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=9)+P(X=10)+P(X=11) \\\\  & =f(9)+f(10)+f(11) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{8})(0.67)^{0}(0.33)^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{8})(0.67)^{1}(0.33)^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{8})(0.67)^{2}(0.33)^{9} \\\\  & ≈0.0013.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>11) & =1-P(X\\le 11) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0013 \\\\  & =0.9987\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661532 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(23,0.66),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{22})(0.34)^{x-23}(0.66)^{23}, & x=23,24,25,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 26.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 26) & =1-P(X<26)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<26) & =P(X\\in \\{23,24,25\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=23)+P(X=24)+P(X=25) \\\\  & =f(23)+f(24)+f(25) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{22}{22})(0.34)^{0}(0.66)^{23}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{23}{22})(0.34)^{1}(0.66)^{23}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{24}{22})(0.34)^{2}(0.66)^{23} \\\\  & ≈0.0029.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 26) & =1-P(X<26) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0029 \\\\  & =0.9971\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661533 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(22,0.68),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{21})(0.32)^{x-22}(0.68)^{22}, & x=22,23,24,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>24.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>24) & =1-P(X\\le 24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 24) & =P(X\\in \\{22,23,24\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=22)+P(X=23)+P(X=24) \\\\  & =f(22)+f(23)+f(24) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{21}{21})(0.32)^{0}(0.68)^{22}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{22}{21})(0.32)^{1}(0.68)^{22}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{23}{21})(0.32)^{2}(0.68)^{22} \\\\  & ≈0.0070.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>24) & =1-P(X\\le 24) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0070 \\\\  & =0.9930\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661551 "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)({y}^{3}+1)}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{2{x}^{2}}\\cdot 3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186661553 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =(1-{e}^{-4x})\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{y}^{2}}(1-{e}^{-4x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{y}^{2}}(1-{e}^{-4x}), & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186661554 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(20,0.85),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{19})(0.15)^{x-20}(0.85)^{20}, & x=20,21,22,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 21) & =P(X\\in \\{20,21\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=20)+P(X=21) \\\\  & =f(20)+f(21) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{19}{19})(0.15)^{0}(0.85)^{20}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{20}{19})(0.15)^{1}(0.85)^{20} \\\\  & ≈0.1550\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661559 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(14,0.63),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{13})(0.37)^{x-14}(0.63)^{14}, & x=14,15,16,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(18\\le X<20) & =P(X\\in \\{18,19\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=18)+P(X=19) \\\\  & =f(18)+f(19) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{17}{13})(0.37)^{4}(0.63)^{14}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{18}{13})(0.37)^{5}(0.63)^{14} \\\\  & ≈0.1614\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661563 "For a negative binomial random variable $X∼\\text{NB}(r,p),$ we have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{r-1})(1-p)^{x-r}{p}^{r}, & x=r,r+1,r+2,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{NB}(9,0.7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{x-1}{8})(0.3)^{x-9}(0.7)^{9}, & x=9,10,11,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(11<X\\le 14) & =P(X\\in \\{12,13,14\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=12)+P(X=13)+P(X=14) \\\\  & =f(12)+f(13)+f(14) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{11}{8})(0.3)^{3}(0.7)^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{8})(0.3)^{4}(0.7)^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{13}{8})(0.3)^{5}(0.7)^{9} \\\\  & ≈0.4678\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."] [17592186661567 "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent continuous random variables with marginal CDFs ${F}_{X}(x)$ and ${F}_{Y}(y),$ respectively, then the corresponding joint CDF of $F(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the joint CDF for our random variables is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & ={F}_{X}(x)\\cdot {F}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}x(x+2)\\cdot {y}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}x(x+2){y}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the joint CDF is\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\text{ or }y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x(x+2){y}^{5}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x(x+2), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>1 \\\\ {y}^{5}, & x>1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>1\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
